From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:56:02 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 15/42] hw/input/tsc2100: Play and record audio

The TSC2100 is the Chumby Classic's audio codec as well as its
touchscreen controller: the stock kernel's chumby-tsc2100 ALSA driver
(also /dev/dsp through the OSS emulation) programs its sample rate as
Fsref (48 or 44.1 kHz, Audio Control 3) divided by the DACFS field of
Audio Control 1, its volume and mutes in the DAC volume register and
its power in Power Control, and runs it as the master of the I2S link
to SSI1. The control panel keeps a 44.1 kHz stream open from boot.

Make the model the master of a serial audio link that the board
provides: while powered up it clocks frames at the DAC's rate through
the link a millisecond at a time, hands those it plays to the audio
backend as 16-bit stereo at that rate, with the DAC's volume and mutes,
and feeds the link the backend's recording at the same rate (silence
when the backend has none). The backend voices are only active while
something drives (takes) the link.

On the Chumby Classic, connect the link to the AUDMUX's peripheral
ports (the codec's DIN on port 2 and DOUT on port 1, as the stock
driver routes them) and add the usual "audiodev" machine property.

Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B1fN5Fc77Z6fVVe4rWJxPT
